## Un formulaire
{% endverbatim %}

{# A form, because half of what a Preset decides is what an input looks like -- its border,
   its corner, its focus ring, the gap between a label and its field -- and none of that is
   visible anywhere else in this gallery. Markup rather than an `{{action}}`: every action
   that renders a form needs a form definition and entries to point at, which a wiki being
   set up for the first time has none of. #}
<div class="yw-form-row">
  <div class="yw-form-group">
    <label class="yw-form-label" for="yw-preview-text">Un champ texte</label>
    <input class="yw-input" id="yw-preview-text" type="text" value="Une valeur saisie" readonly>
  </div>
  <div class="yw-form-group">
    <label class="yw-form-label" for="yw-preview-select">Une liste</label>
    <select class="yw-input" id="yw-preview-select">
      <option>Premier choix</option>
      <option>Deuxième choix</option>
    </select>
  </div>
</div>
{# No `<textarea>` here, deliberately: the formatter escapes one, as it must -- wiki content
   that could inject a text area into a page is content that could rewrite the form around
   it. It shares `.yw-input` with the fields above, so what it would show is already shown. #}
<div class="yw-form-group">
  <label class="yw-form-label" for="yw-preview-disabled">Un champ désactivé</label>
  <input class="yw-input" id="yw-preview-disabled" type="text" value="Non modifiable" disabled>
</div>
<div class="yw-form-group">
  <label class="yw-form-label"><input class="yw-input" type="checkbox" checked> Une case cochée</label>
</div>

<div class="yw-alert yw-alert--success">Un message de succès&nbsp;: son fond et son encre sont calculés à partir de la seule couleur «&nbsp;succès&nbsp;».</div>
<div class="yw-alert yw-alert--info">Un message d'information.</div>
<div class="yw-alert yw-alert--warning">Un avertissement.</div>
<div class="yw-alert yw-alert--danger">Une erreur.</div>

{# The three ways a list of entries is drawn, rendered through the SAME templates a page
   uses -- `@core/presentations/*` -- rather than hand-written markup that would agree with
   itself and with nothing else. The items are fabricated here on purpose: an entry list
   needs a form and entries, and this screen is exactly where a wiki that has neither is
   being set up. Six of them, so a card grid wraps onto a second row and the gap between
   rows is visible, and only four carry an image -- a row mixing cards that have one with
   cards that do not is where a card grid's alignment actually breaks.

   The images are inline SVG data URIs rather than files: a flat rectangle is all that is
   needed to see a card's shape, and nothing here should depend on an upload existing. Their
   colours are literals on purpose -- they stand in for a photograph somebody uploaded, which
   is precisely the thing a Preset does NOT get to recolour, and judging a preset against
   images that obligingly match it would be judging it against nothing. #}
